"""Serialize manually authored reviews of the fully read K34-09..12 leaves.

No verdict or mathematical classification is inferred by this program. Codex
notes are source leads and do not add rows to NOTES_INDEX.tsv.
"""
from pathlib import Path
import json,csv
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
O=ROOT/'deliverable/shards/root_codex';O.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
PAGES=json.loads((ROOT/'work/pages.json').read_text())
claims=[]
def C(n,scans,his,statement,status,cls,fate,refs,search,proof=None,date=None,confidence='high',authorship='Grothendieck handwritten working text'):
    loc=[dict(file=p['file'],cote=34,scan=int(p['scan']),start=p['start'],end=p['end']) for p in PAGES if p['file'] in [f'codex/K34-{i:02}_transcription.tex' for i in range(9,13)] and int(p['scan']) in scans]
    comp={'kind':'Conceptual construction; no numerical computation asserted on these leaves.','review':'Full source passage read; the fate field states the exact extent of the published comparison.'}
    if proof:comp={'kind':'Symbolic re-derivation, with finite consistency calculations where appropriate','proof':'shards/root_codex/proofs.md#'+proof,'code':'code/root_codex_compute.py','output':'outputs/root_codex_computations.json','scope':'Universal arguments are in the proof note; finite calculations check signs, indices or examples only.'}
    claims.append(dict(id=f'C-SA13-{n:03}',items=[],cote=[34],scans=[f'34:{s:03}' for s in scans],lines=loc,his_statement=his,statement=statement,status_on_pages=status,computation=comp,**{'class':cls},fate=fate,fate_date=date or 'Manuscript date not inferred from scan order; publication dates and documented lecture dates are distinguished in references.',present_status='As of 2026-11-19: '+fate,refs=refs,confidence=confidence,search_ids=[search],source_authorship=authorship,source_lead='Codex K34-09..12; no indexed Pro verdict is created.'))

C(600,[149],'5.1: hypothèses en toutes générisations strictes; passage à la limite.',
 'For a strict local noetherian X with closed point x and constant torsion coefficients invertible on X, étale depth at x≥m+1 plus the asserted vanishing on all strict generizations permits the filtered-colimit localization argument to kill the indicated local cohomology groups through degree m. The all-generizations hypothesis cannot be replaced by a stalk assertion only at x.',
 'proof sketched','A','The same induction mechanism is in SGA7 I4.5 proof(1),4.8,4.10–11, under their explicit trait/depth hypotheses. The manuscript proof is a localization argument, not a new general base-change theorem.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_SGA2'],'RC-S01','localization')
C(601,[149,150],'Th.5.2; conclusion Φ_x^i=0 pour i≤m′.',
 'For a morphism to the strict trait of the standing setup, with bad locus Z, the stated rectified-depth bounds and vanishing of the manuscript Φ on strict generizations outside Z imply Φ_x^i=0 for i≤m′. Properness then gives the displayed specialization isomorphisms for i<m′ and injectivity at i=m′. Manuscript Φ is usual RΦ[-1].',
 'proof sketched','A','SGA7 I4.8,4.10–11 retain the depth/generization method. Proper specialization follows from the vanishing-cycle triangle. The source’s weaker end-degree conclusion is retained; no stronger boundary is silently substituted.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S01','localization')
C(602,[150,151,152,153],'Cor.5.3 rewritten; m′=prof géom* X_0, with −d deleted; proof retains −d.',
 'The revised corollary applies the preceding depth criterion with a singular-locus dimension loss. The two surviving formulas for m′ disagree after a cancellation. Consequently a single strongest numerical bound cannot be attributed to the manuscript. Under the checked local complete-intersection trait hypotheses, the conventional usual-RΦ bound is i<n−d.',
 'corrected by himself later','C','SGA7 I4.5–4.9 gives the precise local-complete-intersection/relative-depth conclusions. The cancellation on151 and surviving −d on152 require the scan; neither is silently chosen as the intended theorem.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_SGA2'],'RC-S01',confidence='medium')
C(603,[153],'5.3.3(i): prof géom A_p ≥ prof géom A − dim(A/p).',
 'For the noetherian local rings in the geometric-depth setup, localization lowers geometric depth by at most dim(A/p). Here geometric depth is defined using a complete regular local presentation and the minimal number of generators of its defining ideal; it is not ordinary depth and not a definition using arithmetic rank.',
 'open on the pages','C','SGA2 XIV5.3–5.6 supplies the precise invariant and comparison with étale depth. The exact all-noetherian localization inequality of this lemma was not matched to a fully identical theorem in the sources checked; the lci case follows directly from dimension. The page asks for proofs.', ['RC_SGA2','RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S02',confidence='medium')
C(604,[153],'5.3.3(ii); Donner démonstrations! condition ... peut-être inutile.',
 'For a flat local map A→B of noetherian local rings, the page proposes profgeom(B)≥profgeom(A)+profgeom(B/m_AB), with an extra hypothesis that the residue extension K/k is the residue field of a formally smooth noetherian k-algebra. The doubt concerns removal of that extra hypothesis, not a claim already proved without it.',
 'open on the pages','C','SGA2 XIV5 and SGA7 I4.5 establish the depth framework and the relative complete-intersection subcase. No exact theorem removing this residue-extension hypothesis in the full stated generality was located. This is a bounded negative search result, not a declaration of a new open conjecture.', ['RC_SGA2','RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S02',confidence='medium')
C(605,[154],'Cor.5.4: [n−d+1,n+1]; global [n−d+1,n+d+1].',
 'For a flat local-complete-intersection family over the standing trait, pure relative dimension n and singular locus in the special fibre of dimension≤d, manuscript Φ has sheaf degrees in [n−d+1,n+1] and, for the proper support calculation, hypercohomology degrees in [n−d+1,n+d+1]. For isolated singularities it is concentrated in degree n+1. Translate by one to usual vanishing cycles.',
 'proof sketched','A','SGA7 I4.5–4.8 proves the corresponding usual-φ lower bound and isolated lci concentration under the published characteristic-zero trait assumptions; the upper bound is the affine cohomological bound. The manuscript’s broader coefficient/trait range is not inferred from that publication alone.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S01','amplitudes',confidence='medium')
C(606,[154],'inertie triviale ... i≠n; spécialisation sauf n,n+1.',
 'For the proper isolated-singularity lci situation in C-SA13-605, specialization is an isomorphism away from middle degrees n,n+1, and inertia on generic-fibre cohomology is trivial for i≠n. The statement uses the exact sequence and the shifted concentration, not a general claim that every singular degeneration has trivial nonmiddle monodromy.',
 'proof sketched','A','This is the immediate long-exact-sequence consequence of SGA7 I4.6 and proper nearby-cycle comparison. Its full hypotheses are those recorded in C-SA13-605.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S01','localization')
C(607,[154,155],'5.5–5.7; H⁰,H¹ non commutatif; SGA2 XIV6.3.',
 'The low-degree analogue proposes connectedness and prime-to-p torsor specialization under the corresponding homotopical-depth bounds, in particular for a flat lci family whose nonsmooth locus has the stated codimension≥2. Which ambient space carries that codimension must be checked. The extension is explicitly conditional on a local Lefschetz/homotopical-depth input.',
 'open on the pages','C','Michèle Raynaud1974 III1.2–1.3 proves H⁰/H¹ comparison under specified depth, ind-finiteness and base hypotheses. Orgogozo2003 removes resolution assumptions in several prime-to-p generic-acyclicity and fundamental-group results. Neither source was treated as an automatic proof of every excellent-base variant in this marginal sentence.', ['RC_MRaynaud1974','RC_Orgogozo2003','RC_SGA2'],'RC-S03',confidence='medium')
C(608,[156],'m−d−1 acyclique; m−d≥2 ... 1-asphérique.',
 'For a flat map of excellent strict local noetherian schemes, after controlling the dimension d of the geometric singular locus under finite residue extensions, the page proposes local prime-to-p (m−d−1)-acyclicity from geometric depth m, and a nonabelian degree-one assertion when m−d≥2. Its proof invokes resolution and conjectural local Lefschetz input.',
 'open on the pages','C','The trait lci case is SGA7 I4.5–4.8; the checked nonabelian comparison is Raynaud1974 III1.2–1.3, with its actual base restrictions, and Orgogozo2003 handles generic prime-to-p acyclicity. The exact arbitrary excellent local-base formulation was not identified as a single unconditional theorem in the searches.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_MRaynaud1974','RC_Orgogozo2003'],'RC-S03',confidence='medium')
C(609,[157,158],'6.2: R→K→L; on retrouve les quatre espaces.',
 'For the strict-local degeneration with nonsmoothness confined to x, write K=RΓ(V_barη,Λ), L=RΓ(V_s−{x},Λ). The specialization/nearby-cycle morphism, with its inertia-equivariant structure and cone, packages the cohomology of the punctured total space, its generic part, punctured special fibre and geometric generic fibre. Recovering the generic part uses derived inertia invariants, not ordinary invariants.',
 'proof sketched','A','The nearby-cycle triangle and proper/local comparisons are the published SGA7 formalism, reviewed in Illusie2021 §1. The exact four-space reconstruction here is independently checked as a cone/localization diagram; it is not attributed to a separate numbered theorem in that review.', ['RC_Illusie2021','RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S04','nearby-cones')
C(610,[158],'6.2.4: RΓ(I,Λ)=Λ⊕Λ[-1], Tate twist absent in one line.',
 'For ℓ-primary coefficients with ℓ≠p and trivial geometric inertia action, H⁰(I,Λ)=Λ and H¹(I,Λ)=Λ(−1), higher groups zero; after choosing a tame generator the underlying two-term complex splits. The untwisted direct sum is not canonical over a nontrivial residue Galois group. Apply this to L with trivial I-action.',
 'stated without proof','E','The continuous-cohomology two-term resolution gives the corrected expression. SGA7 I0.3 records the cyclotomic action on tame inertia. A generator trivializes the twist only after making a choice.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S04','inertia-cohomology')
C(611,[159,160],'6.3,6.4: pureté; β; b) laissé pour plus tard.',
 'Absolute purity for the regular codimension-one special fibre gives i!Λ=Λ(−1)[−2]. The localization triangle yields the two cone identifications of6.4, with the sign of the boundary fixed by the triangle convention. Part(a) is sketched; part(b) and one sign are explicitly left to check.',
 'open on the pages','A','The codimension-one purity and nearby-cycle formalism are standard published ingredients. This run checks the formal cone/degrees once a coherent sign convention is fixed; it does not decide the ink of the boundary β. The later SGA7 formalism supplies the correct convention, rather than certifying an unread sign.', ['RC_Illusie2021','RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S04','nearby-cones')
C(612,[161],'H^i(V_barη)=0 pour i>n; suite de Hochschild–Serre.',
 'For the affine geometric generic fibre of dimension n in the standing excellent trait setup and torsion coefficients prime to p, H^i=0 for i>n. Inertia has ℓ-cd1, so 0→H^{i−1}(V_barη)_I(−1)→H^i(V_η)→H^i(V_barη)^I→0, and H^i(V_η)=0 for i>n+1. The coinvariant Tate twist is essential equivariantly.',
 'proof sketched','A','The affine vanishing and inertia spectral sequence used in SGA7 I4 are the published counterparts. The edge sequence and upper bound are rederived here, with the omitted twist marked as a scan alarm.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S04','inertia-cohomology')
C(613,[161,162],'Gysin isomorphisme au-dessus de n; Λ(−n±1).',
 'The same localization sequence gives the high-degree Gysin isomorphisms after the affine vanishing range. For a regular strict local space of dimension n, the punctured top cohomology is H^{2n−1}=Λ(−n); for dimension n+1 it is H^{2n+1}=Λ(−n−1). These are comparison cases, not a claim that every singular punctured lci is a cohomology sphere.',
 'proof sketched','A','Purity fixes the top-degree twist and the degree shift. The transcription’s uncertain −n±1 must be compared with the dimension of the actual ambient local space before deciding the reading.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S04','nearby-cones')
C(614,[164],'plus grand quotient premier à p; sous-groupe engendré par les p-Sylow.',
 'For a profinite group Π, Π^(p′) is the quotient by the closed normal subgroup generated by its pro-p Sylow subgroups; it is universal among continuous maps to pro-prime-to-p groups. This construction is functorial.',
 'proof sketched','E','The finite-quotient argument proves the description and passes to the profinite inverse limit. The same quotient is used in SGA7 I5.2 and Orgogozo2003.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Orgogozo2003'],'RC-S05','prime-to-p')
C(615,[165,166],'1.1: suite exacte; action extérieure; point rationnel.',
 'For a geometrically connected finite-type K-scheme X and chosen geometric point, 1→π1(X_Kbar)→π1(X)→G_K→1 defines an outer action. A K-rational point with the corresponding base point supplies a section and hence an actual action. Passing to the geometric maximal prime-to-p quotient preserves the relevant exact extension.',
 'proof sketched','A','Exact published match: SGA7 I5.2, including the rational-point section. The geometric-connectedness hypothesis cannot be replaced by connectedness alone.', ['RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S05','prime-to-p',date='SGA7 I§5 reports lecture1968-11-19; publication1972.')
C(616,[166],'Aut, Int, AutExt comme groupes profinis.',
 'For topologically finitely generated profinite π, the automorphism group with its inverse-limit topology over characteristic finite quotients is profinite; Inn and Out carry the corresponding compact quotient structures. The unqualified assertion for arbitrary profinite π needs an additional finiteness/topology hypothesis.',
 'stated without proof','C','The finitely generated situation is the one needed by the following monodromy argument and follows from characteristic finite quotients. The page’s general wording is not justified for arbitrary profinite groups. No blanket compactness claim is retained.', ['RC_Orgogozo2003','RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S05','prime-to-p',confidence='medium')
C(617,[167],'Th.1.3: action ... potentiellement modérée.',
 'For a geometrically connected finite-type scheme over the fraction field of a strict henselian trait, wild inertia has finite image in Out(π1^(p′)); with a rational base point the same holds in Aut. Thus after a finite extension the action is tame. The equivalence uses that Inn(π1^(p′)) is pro-prime-to-p.',
 'proof sketched','A','Exact published theorem: SGA7 I5.3, with the same normalization, path and curve-reduction proof. This is established, not an anabelian open problem.', ['RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S05','prime-to-p',date='Lecture1968-11-19; publication1972.')
C(618,[167],'1.3.1: présentation finie; Murre; résolution.',
 'The maximal prime-to-p quotient of the étale fundamental group of a finite-type scheme over a separably closed field is finitely presented as a pro-prime-to-p group. The draft invokes a resolution-dependent route for nonproper schemes.',
 'stated without proof','B','Orgogozo2003 Theorem4.11 p143 proves this without the old resolution hypothesis, using alterations. The exact field and finite-type quantifiers match.', ['RC_Orgogozo2003'],'RC-S05',date='Received2002-01-25; accepted2002-03-22; publication2003. No earlier public circulation established.')
C(619,[168],'Th.1.4: image abélienne et première à p; rang ≤ nombre de points doubles.',
 'For a proper flat nodal marked curve over a strict local base, disjoint marked sections in the smooth locus, and a base-point section in their complement, monodromy over the smooth-base open has abelian prime-to-p image; in the universal deformation it is generated by the node parameters. The draft also announces a higher-dimensional projective-pair version using hyperplane sections.',
 'proof sketched','A','Exact curve theorem: SGA7 I5.4; the higher-dimensional analogue is explicitly Remark5.5 rather than a separately proved full theorem there. The rank bound follows from the number of normal-crossing deformation parameters in the argument.', ['RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S06','kummer',confidence='medium',date='Lecture1968-11-19; publication1972.')
C(620,[170,172,173,174],'normalisation; chemins; H¹(P,π^(p′))=1.',
 'Finite normalization descent gives a van Kampen presentation from components and their intersections. The prime-to-p path torsors between chosen rational points admit a wild-inertia-fixed point: a pro-p group acting on a profinite torsor of prime-to-p finite cardinalities has compatible fixed points. This removes the path obstruction for wild inertia, not for all inertia.',
 'proof sketched','A','SGA7 I5.3.1 uses precisely this fixed-path argument. The compactness step and finite orbit count are independently supplied; the erased “Nous admettrons” on172 is not treated as a surviving admission.', ['RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S06','fixed-paths')
C(621,[175,176],'réduction affine lisse puis courbe; réduction stable admise.',
 'For a normal integral geometric fibre, restriction from a dense open induces a surjection on π1. A generic hyperplane reduction reduces the prime-to-p monodromy problem to a smooth affine curve; after finite field extension, stable reduction and blow-ups separate the marked sections and put them in the smooth locus.',
 'proof sketched','A','SGA7 I5.1.1 and5.3.2 provide the published reduction, explicitly using stable reduction. The page does not itself prove the stable-reduction theorem or solve its imperfect-residue-field worry.', ['RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S06')
C(622,[177],'Bertini avec section; le carré devrait suffire; EGA V.',
 'For a sufficiently ample embedding, seek a smooth generic hyperplane curve through the prescribed section with the required π1 surjectivity and boundary transversality. The page wonders whether a square of the chosen ample bundle already suffices. This fixed exponent is not established by a generic sufficiently-large-power Bertini theorem.',
 'open on the pages','C','SGA7 I5.5 uses the hyperplane reduction without the proposed fixed-power bound. Orgogozo2003 Theorem4.10 proves a prime-to-p Lefschetz theorem in dimension≥3; it does not verify this exact prescribed-section degree-two assertion. EGA V is a program reference, not a published volume.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Orgogozo2003'],'RC-S06',confidence='medium')
C(623,[178],'invariance du π1 premier à p; I′→I surjectif.',
 'Prime-to-p π1 of a finite-type scheme over a separably closed field is invariant under extension of separably closed fields. The group-surjectivity step for a geometric base extension requires the relevant geometric connectedness; connectedness of total space and base alone is insufficient.',
 'stated without proof','B','Orgogozo2003 proves the base-extension invariance unconditionally (Corollary4.5, after the harmless passage from separably to algebraically closed fields). The manuscript’s geometric-connectedness omission is a hypothesis alarm, not a counterexample to that correctly stated result.', ['RC_Orgogozo2003'],'RC-S05','prime-to-p',confidence='medium')
C(624,[179],'Lemme3.1 admis; W[[t_1,...,t_N]], D_i:t_i=0.',
 'After the indicated completion reduction, over a complete noetherian strictly henselian base equipped with a Cohen-ring map, the deformation of a stable marked nodal curve is induced from a formally smooth formal base over that Cohen ring, with independent node-smoothing parameters; the discriminant is the union of the coordinate divisors. The source admits the deformation lemma and points to a later exposé.',
 'stated without proof','A','SGA7 I5.1 and the proof of5.4 use this deformation statement and refer to Schlessinger’s theory in Rim’s published ExposéVI, not the draft’s exposé-number forecast. The deformation input is attributed to Schlessinger/Rim; class A records its explicit use in Grothendieck’s published SGA7 I, not independent authorship of their deformation theorem. Exact formal-base construction is an input here, not independently reproved. The completion reduction is explicit in K34-10 global lines150 and261 before the scan179 construction.', ['RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S06',date='Lecture1968-11-19; publication1972.')
C(625,[180],'S[T]/(T_i^m); revêtements de Kummer.',
 'Over the complement of a strict normal-crossing divisor t_1…t_r=0, with m invertible, Kummer covers adjoin T_i satisfying T_i^m=t_i; their derivative is invertible there. The printed transcription T_i^m=0 would instead give a nilpotent, nonétale algebra. These covers yield the prime-to-p abelian inertia group Zhat^(p′)(1)^r.',
 'proof sketched','A','The argument in SGA7 I5.4 uses Abhyankar’s lemma and the normal-crossing universal base. The missing −t_i is a high-impact scan alarm, not an editorial correction to the supplied transcription.', ['RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S06','kummer')
C(626,[180,181],'h_i commutent; h=∏h_i^{n_i}; Deligne plus tard.',
 'Node loops commute in the outer prime-to-p monodromy image. Pulling the universal deformation back to a trait multiplies the i-th tame Kummer character by the valuation n_i of its smoothing parameter, so the total outer monodromy is the product of the commuting node monodromies to powers n_i. The thickness is length(V/a_iV), not an arbitrary intersection multiplicity.',
 'proof sketched','A','The abelian universal-base argument is SGA7 I5.4. The product formula follows by pulling back Kummer classes; the separately promised transcendental description of each node monodromy belongs to the later Picard–Lefschetz treatment and is not supplied on this page.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S06','kummer')

C(627,[183],'6.1: structure analytique unique; hom continu ... continu.',
 'A topological group admitting a compatible finite-dimensional Q_ell-analytic group structure has that analytic structure uniquely; continuous homomorphisms between such groups are analytic and closed subgroups are analytic. A nondiscrete compact analytic group determines ell. The tautological second “continu” is a likely reading for “analytique”.',
 'stated without proof','E','These are standard p-adic Lie-group results already in the Lazard-era theory. The compact linearity argument is separately checked in C-SA13-636; the manuscript’s tautological word is not treated as a mathematical assertion of automatic analyticity without comparison.', ['RC_Lazard1965','RC_CasalsZozaya2025'],'RC-S07',confidence='medium',date='Lazard1965, before the SGA7-era sheets; first circulation of each standard fact not separately established.')
C(628,[183],'6.2: système projectif des quotients analytiques.',
 'The continuous compact Q_ell-analytic quotients of a profinite group π form a cofiltered pro-object: the image in a product dominates two quotients, and its kernel is the intersection of the two kernels. Every finite-dimensional continuous Q_ell-representation factors through its compact analytic image.',
 'proof sketched','E','Closed-subgroup theory and compactness prove the construction. The denominator written G/(V∩V′) must be π/(V∩V′). This exact pro-object packaging was not identified as a separately published Grothendieck theorem.', ['RC_Lazard1965','RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S07','analytic-envelopes')
C(629,[184],'6.4 fidèle, pas pleinement fidèle; ouverts donnent même pro-Lie.',
 'Differentiation of finite-dimensional continuous representations gives a faithful functor to representations of Lie^an(π), but it need not be full: a nontrivial finite-image character becomes trivial after differentiation. Restriction to an open subgroup leaves the analytic pro-Lie algebra unchanged.',
 'proof sketched','E','The finite-character counterexample and open-subgroup argument are checked directly. They agree with local integration in p-adic Lie theory; no “connected components” of a totally disconnected analytic space are substituted for the algebraic components used later.', ['RC_Lazard1965','RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S07','analytic-envelopes')
C(630,[184,185],'lim_i Mod_Qell(U_i) ≃ Mod g; théorème bien connu.',
 'The filtered colimit of continuous representation categories over open subgroups of π is equivalent to finite-dimensional representations of its analytic pro-Lie algebra. Morphisms and objects are interpreted as germs on a sufficiently small open subgroup; every finite-dimensional Lie-algebra representation integrates after shrinking.',
 'stated without proof','E','Local exponential/Baker–Campbell–Hausdorff integration is the standard input; the pro-system reduction and the germ interpretation are verified here. This is not a global equivalence Rep(π)≃Rep(Lieπ).', ['RC_Lazard1965','RC_CasalsZozaya2025'],'RC-S07','analytic-envelopes')
C(631,[185,186],'6.5: on ne récupère pas π ... seulement enveloppes algébriques.',
 'The tensor category with its forgetful fibre functor reconstructs the proalgebraic envelope. It does not identify the original analytic group with the rational points of that envelope. A literal claim that even the profinite group cannot be recovered conflicts with the next page’s component quotient πalg/(πalg)^0≃π; at least canonical recovery and the role of the fibre functor must be distinguished.',
 'stated without proof','C','Deligne–Milne1982 Proposition2.8,Remark2.10 andTheorem2.11 give precise reconstruction with a fibre functor. The broad warning on185 is not adopted as a theorem negating186. The comparison is a scope correction, pending the scan and exact intended sense of “recover”.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S08','tannakian',confidence='medium')
C(632,[185,186],'πalg=(Zariski closures G_E); équivalence tensorielle.',
 'Take the Zariski closures of all continuous finite-dimensional Q_ell-representation images of π, ordered using finite direct sums and tensor/subquotient comparisons. Their projective limit is an affine proalgebraic group with its tautological fibre functor; its algebraic representation category is the original category of continuous representations of π.',
 'proof sketched','B','The construction is an instance of neutral Tannakian reconstruction, precisely Deligne–Milne1982 Theorem2.11. The source’s quotient-map indexing is completed by direct sums; this completion is our explicit reconstruction, not additional ink.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S08','tannakian',date='General Tannakian development: Saavedra1972 cited by Deligne–Milne; verified theorem in1982, corrected author version2012. No exact first publication of this appendix located.')
C(633,[186],'πalg/(πalg)^0 ≃ π, finite groups viewed as algebraic groups.',
 'The maximal pro-étale component quotient of the continuous proalgebraic envelope over Q_ell is the original profinite π, regarded as an inverse system of finite constant group schemes. Finite continuous representations separate finite quotients, and every component quotient of an image closure is a finite continuous quotient of π.',
 'stated without proof','B','The two-direction finite-quotient argument is independently supplied; it is compatible with Tannakian finite-object detection, Deligne–Milne2.20. This directly limits the preceding warning in C-SA13-631.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S08','tannakian')
C(634,[186,187],'lim_U Mod(U) ≃ Mod((πalg)^0); différentiation pleinement fidèle.',
 'Restriction to sufficiently small open subgroups removes the finite component quotients, yielding the algebraic representation category of the connected proalgebraic envelope. Differentiation from representations of a connected affine algebraic group in characteristic zero is fully faithful, but arbitrary Lie representations need not algebraize.',
 'proof sketched','B','The connected-envelope statement follows from finite algebraic quotients and finite-index restriction; the failure of general algebraization is illustrated by nonintegral weights for Lie(G_m). Deligne–Milne supplies the tensor framework, not an assertion that all Lie modules integrate algebraically.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S08','tannakian')
C(635,[187],'points de Γ/Γ0 rationnels, donc Γ(Qell) dense.',
 'For the particular Zariski closures generated by π, rational points are dense by construction. For an arbitrary affine Q_ell-group Γ, rationality of the component group alone does not ensure rational points in every component. A sufficient formulation is that Γ(Q_ell) meets every geometric component, since the identity component has dense local analytic points.',
 'false as stated','C','The intended image-closure application is valid. A literal general implication is refuted by the normalizer in SL2 of the norm-one torus of Q3(sqrt(3))/Q3: its quotient is constant C2, but its other component would require an element of norm−1, which does not exist. The complete valuation/residue check is in the proof note.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982','RC_Lazard1965'],'RC-S08','rational-components',confidence='medium')
C(636,[188],'tout groupe analytique compact ... représentation fidèle; réf?',
 'Every compact Q_ell-analytic group has a faithful finite-dimensional continuous Q_ell-representation. A faithful Lie-algebra representation integrates on a small open subgroup; induction to the compact group is finite dimensional and remains faithful.',
 'open on the pages','E','The missing reference is supplied by p-adic Lie theory. Casals-Ruiz–Zozaya2025 Theorem2.1 gives a primary proof for uniform pro-p groups and explicitly uses finite-index induction; it cites Lazard’s earlier proof. This is not a newly open linearity question for compact Q_ell-groups.', ['RC_Lazard1965','RC_CasalsZozaya2025'],'RC-S07','analytic-envelopes',date='Lazard1965; modern proof and bound published online2025-03-21. The2025 result over more general pro-p domains is a different extension.')
C(637,[188],'6.6.1: isom de pro-groupes analytiques? jamais sauf ...',
 'The analytic image groups map into their Zariski closures. Identifying the entire analytic pro-object with the algebraic-envelope pro-object viewed on Q_ell-points is substantially stronger than equality of their Lie algebras; algebraic rational-point groups need not be compact. The tentative “never except…” sentence is not a precise theorem on the page.',
 'open on the pages','F','The construction and Lie comparison can be identified, but the tentative exceptional case and the category in which its “isomorphism” is meant remain ambiguous. Searches of the Tannakian and p-adic Lie literature did not locate this wording as a theorem. No invented classification is attached.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982','RC_Lazard1965'],'RC-S08',confidence='low')
C(638,[188],'6.6.2: Liean→Liealg iso ssi g_E algébriques; parfaites suffisent.',
 'For each representation image G_E, equality of its analytic Lie algebra with that of its Zariski closure is equivalent to G_E being analytically open in the Q_ell-points of that closure near the identity. If every image Lie algebra is algebraic, the analytic and algebraic pro-Lie systems coincide. A perfect linear Lie algebra in characteristic zero is algebraic, giving the stated sufficient case.',
 'proof sketched','E','These are classical Lie/algebraic-envelope facts; the finite-dimensional openness equivalence is independently checked by equal dimensions and the inverse-function theorem. The assertion concerns all representation images, not just one chosen faithful representation.', ['RC_Lazard1965','RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S08','analytic-envelopes',confidence='medium')
C(639,[188,189],'Remarque6.7: revêtement simplement connexe; laisser tomber.',
 'For a semisimple algebraic image, passing to sufficiently small compact open subgroups allows representations of the simply connected algebraic cover to occur in the representation system. This explains why the limit has more representations than a fixed non-simply-connected image. The remark is marked for omission.',
 'corrected by himself later','E','Local lifting through a central isogeny in characteristic zero and induction give the explanation. The marginal “laisser tomber” is an editorial withdrawal, not evidence that the local lifting observation is false. It is not counted as a surviving research conjecture.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982','RC_Lazard1965'],'RC-S08','analytic-envelopes')
C(640,[189,190],'Remarque6.8: extension, centre, ξ∈H²; laisser tomber.',
 'The discarded discussion proposes describing the envelope of a compact open subgroup of an algebraic group using a connected cover and a central extension class. For an actual central extension 1→Z→E→π→1 with the specified continuous action, its class in continuous H²(π,Z) vanishes exactly when a continuous splitting exists. The additional Borel-fixed lifting and centre identifications are unstable in the overwritten text.',
 'corrected by himself later','F','The general central-extension criterion is standard, but no unique full theorem can be extracted from the cancelled/overwritten group and centre choices. Tannakian reconstruction does not by itself validate the displayed Borel-lifting equivalence. Preserve as withdrawn working material with a scan alarm.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S08',confidence='low')
C(641,[191],'6.9: fonctorialité; image d’indice fini → épimorphismes.',
 'A continuous homomorphism of profinite groups induces analytic and algebraic envelope morphisms and their Lie morphisms. A surjection stays surjective on finite image quotients; finite-index image gives surjections on connected algebraic envelopes and both pro-Lie algebras. The topology and scheme-theoretic image are kept separate.',
 'proof sketched','B','The image-closure and finite-index arguments prove the assertions at every finite-dimensional quotient; Tannakian faithful-flatness is characterized by Deligne–Milne2.21. Diagram-arrow ambiguities are listed for scan review.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S08','tannakian')
C(642,[191,192],'6.10: Π≠lim π_lambda; exemples6.10.1,6.10.2.',
 'Applying envelopes levelwise to a pro-object of profinite groups need not commute with taking its inverse-limit topological group. For Π=(Z/ell^n), every level has zero Lie algebra while limΠ=Z_ell has one-dimensional analytic Lie algebra. For Π=(ell^n Z_ell) under inclusions, limΠ=0 while all transition Lie maps are isomorphisms.',
 'proof sketched','E','Both examples are verified directly, including the inverse limits and the nonzero Lie system. They support the manuscript’s distinction between a pro-object and its limit; no external-project interpretation is needed.', ['RC_Lazard1965','RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S09','pro-objects')
C(643,[193],'6.11–6.12: colim catégories; quasi-surjectif; pro-objet strict.',
 'The representation category of the levelwise envelope of Π=(π_lambda) is the filtered colimit of finite-dimensional continuous representation categories at finite levels. If transitions have finite-index image, the connected algebraic and both Lie systems have surjective transition maps, so they are strict pro-objects.',
 'proof sketched','B','This follows from finite presentation of each representation datum and C-SA13-641. The formalism is compatible with Tannakian reconstruction; the finite-index hypothesis is essential to the strictness assertion.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S09','pro-objects')
C(644,[193,194],'6.13: X=lim X_lambda; foncteur limite pleinement fidèle.',
 'For cofiltered systems of finite-type Z-schemes with affine transition maps in the qcqs finite-presentation setting, the limiting scheme determines its model pro-object, by the finite-presentation limit property for morphisms. The associated pro-group Π=(π1(X_lambda)) classifies lisse Q_ell-sheaves defined at some model. This is not an assertion that every lisse Q_ell-sheaf on X descends to one model.',
 'stated without proof','A','The finite-presentation limit formalism is the EGA/SGA foundation of spreading out; the exact modern limit statements are in Stacks Limits of Schemes and Fundamental Groups. The categorical claim is restricted here to the qcqs hypotheses under which that formalism applies.', ['RC_StacksLimits','RC_StacksFundamental'],'RC-S09','pro-objects',confidence='medium')
C(645,[194],'π1(X)=lim π1(X_lambda), mais Π non déterminé par ce groupe.',
 'Finite étale covers descend along affine qcqs limits, giving π1(X)=limπ1(X_lambda). The inverse-limit group need not determine the original pro-object. For X=Spec(F_pbar), ordinary π1 is trivial, while the arithmetic model system over finite fields retains nontrivial germs of ell-adic representations.',
 'proof sketched','A','The finite-étale limit theorem is standard SGA1/EGA and is checked through Stacks. The nontrivial model-system example is independently computed in C-SA13-647–648.', ['RC_StacksFundamental','RC_StacksLimits'],'RC-S09','pro-objects')
C(646,[195],'6.14: modèles normaux intègres; transition quasi-surjective.',
 'For normal integral X in the model setup, normalize the schematic images to obtain normal integral finite-type models. Dominant finite-type maps between normal integral schemes induce finite-index images on π1; hence the associated connected envelopes and Lie systems have surjective transitions.',
 'proof sketched','A','Normality and finite type control the algebraic part of the function-field extension and the finite étale covers. This is the standard finite-index image argument, checked here at the field/normalization level; the source’s pro-Lie conclusion follows from C-SA13-641.', ['RC_StacksFundamental','RC_StacksLimits'],'RC-S09','pro-objects',confidence='medium')
C(647,[195,196],'6.15: dim g>0 ... ces dimensions toujours infinies.',
 'For nonempty normal integral X in the stated arithmetic-model setup with ell invertible, the arithmetic system has a nontrivial analytic Lie quotient. The stronger parenthesis that both analytic and algebraic dimensions are always infinite is false: for X=Spec(F_pbar), finite-field models are cofinal and Lie^anΠ is a one-dimensional constant Q_ell-system.',
 'false as stated','C','The finite-field counterexample checks normality, integrality, ell invertibility, cofinality of models and the transition Lie maps. It refutes only the analytic infinite-dimensional assertion; the algebraic-envelope assertion survives as C-SA13-648. No scan-only repair is presumed.', ['RC_Lazard1965','RC_DeligneMilne1982','RC_StacksFundamental'],'RC-S09','arithmetic-dimensions')
C(648,[196],'Zhat^alg et Z_ell^alg de dimension infinie.',
 'The continuous Q_ell-proalgebraic envelopes of Z_ell and Zhat have algebraic quotients of arbitrarily large dimension. Choose arbitrarily many Q-linearly independent ell-adic exponents in a small convergence ball; the corresponding analytic characters have Zariski-dense joint image in a torus of that rank. Their analytic image still has dimension one.',
 'stated without proof','B','The elementary character argument gives the precise distinction required by the preceding counterexample. Tannakian reconstruction identifies these quotients as quotients of the envelope. Infinite algebraic dimension is not evidence of infinite analytic dimension.', ['RC_DeligneMilne1982','RC_Lazard1965'],'RC-S09','arithmetic-dimensions')
C(649,[196],'cas p>0 ... Spec F_p; autre cas Spec Z[1/ell].',
 'In positive characteristic, the arithmetic quotient over a finite field supplies Zhat. In characteristic zero, the cyclotomic tower unramified outside ell supplies a Z_ell^× quotient of π1(Spec Z[1/ell]); the draft labels both branches p>0. For the claimed maximal abelian quotient one uses the Kronecker–Weber conductor theorem, not merely the existence of cyclotomic covers.',
 'proof sketched','E','The cyclotomic construction suffices for the nonzero Lie quotient. The duplicated characteristic label is a scan alarm. The classical maximal-abelian identification is cited only to the checked primary exposition of class field theory; it is not derived from the one-line manuscript argument.', ['RC_MilneCFT','RC_StacksFundamental'],'RC-S10','arithmetic-dimensions')
C(650,[196,197],'6.16: faisceaux qui se rencontrent pratiquement; R^if_*; correspondances.',
 'A proper smooth morphism of finite presentation, with ell invertible, and a finite collection of algebraic correspondences spread out to sufficiently fine arithmetic models; their lisse higher direct images and induced maps then lie in the model representation category. The informal word “all” means these geometric constructions, not all possible continuous ell-adic local systems.',
 'proof sketched','A','Finite-presentation descent and proper smooth base change establish the specified constructions. The manuscript’s claim about everything encountered in practice is interpreted as a programmatic restriction of the category, not a theorem on all local systems.', ['RC_StacksLimits','RC_StacksFundamental','RC_SGA7I'],'RC-S09','pro-objects')
C(651,[197],'Hom(T_ell(A),Z_ell) ≃ R¹f_*Z_ell; tensoriser d’abord.',
 'For an abelian scheme A→X and ell invertible on X, the dual of its Tate-module local system is R¹f_*Z_ell. Tensor with Q_ell before differentiating to obtain a representation of the arithmetic analytic or algebraic pro-Lie algebra.',
 'stated without proof','E','This standard degree-one comparison is used in the Tate endomorphism question. It is not a canonical self-duality without a polarization; the source correctly uses the dual.', ['RC_Tate1966','RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S10')
C(652,[198],'End(A)⊗Qell injecte dans End_Π(E); bijectif conjectures de Tate.',
 'For an abelian scheme over a normal integral base in the arithmetic-model setup, the natural map from End(A)⊗Q_ell to endomorphisms of V_ell(A) commuting with the arithmetic pro-group is injective; the page expects surjectivity. On a finitely generated field this is the Tate homomorphism theorem. Geometric algebraically closed bases still retain arithmetic model germs, so replacing Π by ordinary π1 would give the wrong claim.',
 'open on the pages','B','The finite-field case is Tate1966; the characteristic-zero finitely generated field theorem is Faltings, and positive-characteristic analogues are due to Zarhin/Mori. The extension from fields to the displayed normal-base/model formulation uses descent of homomorphisms and normal-base extension, and is explicitly a reconstruction. Exact sourced scope is in the reference audit.', ['RC_Tate1966','RC_Faltings1983','RC_Zarhin2010'],'RC-S10','tate-centralizers',confidence='medium',date='Tate1966; Faltings1983. Positive-characteristic first dates are recorded only when an exact primary locator is obtained; no single publication date assigned to the model-progroup reformulation.')
C(653,[199],'End_GE(E)=End_GtildeE(E); si π1(X)=1, End_Lie aussi.',
 'An endomorphism commutes with a linear group iff it commutes with its Zariski closure, since commutation is a polynomial condition. When the arithmetic image has connected algebraic closure, its centralizer also equals that of its Lie algebra in characteristic zero. In the model system, trivial ordinary π1 removes finite component obstructions only after passing to a sufficiently fine model.',
 'proof sketched','B','The polynomial centralizer identity is independently checked. The connected-group/Lie identity is standard and appears in the Lie-centralizer method used by Zarhin2010. The finite-model qualification prevents treating every single arithmetic model image as connected.', ['RC_Zarhin2010','RC_DeligneMilne1982'],'RC-S10','tate-centralizers')

C(654,[201],'E2^{pq}=H^p(σ↦H^q(Y_σ)); poids q; d_r nulle.',
 'For a proper strict normal-crossing variety over a finite field whose components and all intersections are smooth projective, the Čech spectral sequence E1^{p,q}=⊕H^q(Y_{α0…αp},Q_ell) converges to H^{p+q}(Y). E2^{p,q} is pure of weight q, so d_r=0 for r≥2. Thus H^i(Y) has weights among0,…,i. The page’s “all r” starts at its displayed E2 page.',
 'proof sketched','B','Deligne’s Weil purity makes the conditional weight calculation unconditional in this finite-field setup; the spectral weight comparison is independently redone. This does not itself give ell-independent ranks of the restriction maps in the Čech complex.', ['RC_WeilII1980'],'RC-S11','weights')
C(655,[201,203],'via conjectures standard, dimensions indépendantes de ell.',
 'The dimensions of the Čech cohomology pieces and resulting weight pieces are asserted to be independent of ell under the standard conjectures controlling algebraic correspondences and their ranks. Purity alone controls weights, not the ranks of all algebraic restriction/Gysin maps.',
 'open on the pages','C','Weight degeneration is established; the conditional motivic/rank assertion is distinct. No unconditional theorem covering every stated normal-crossing complex over every finite field was identified in the checked sources. The dossier does not convert the general standard conjectures into solved facts.', ['RC_WeilII1980','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S11',confidence='medium')
C(656,[201,203],'Ker et Coker φ^i indépendants de la résolution; morceaux par poids.',
 'For U=Xbar−Y with Xbar smooth projective and Y strict normal crossings over a finite field, the compact-support localization sequence identifies ker[Hc^i(U)→H^i(Xbar)] with W_{i−1}Hc^i(U), kerφ^i with Gr_i^W Hc^i(U), and cokerφ^i with W_i Hc^{i+1}(U), where φ^i:H^i(Xbar)→H^i(Y). These descriptions make the objects independent of the chosen good compactification.',
 'proof sketched','B','The mixed-weight formalism in WeilII gives the strict weight separation; the exact-sequence argument is supplied here. Over characteristic zero the corresponding mixed-Hodge statement is a separate realization. Existence of a good compactification is an assumption, not inferred in arbitrary characteristic.', ['RC_WeilII1980','RC_HodgeIII1974'],'RC-S11','weights')
C(657,[203],'Im φ^i ∩ Filt¹=0; filtration quotient sur Coker.',
 'The image of the pure weight-i cohomology of Xbar in H^i(Y) meets the lower-weight part trivially. Its map to the Čech E2^{0,i} term is injective; the other Čech terms give the lower-weight filtration of cokerφ^i. A strictness statement for weights, not a chosen direct-sum decomposition, is what makes this canonical.',
 'proof sketched','B','This follows from the verified weights and exact sequence in C-SA13-654,656. No splitting of mixed Frobenius modules is asserted.', ['RC_WeilII1980'],'RC-S11','weights')
C(658,[203],'H_Y^q=∧^{q−1}(⊕Qell_Yα(−1)); last sum has (−1).',
 'For a strict normal-crossing divisor Y in a smooth scheme Xbar and q≥2, the local cohomology sheaf H_Y^q(Q_ell) identifies with R^{q−1}j_*Q_ell and with the (q−1)-st exterior power of the divisor Kummer sheaf. Each (q−1)-fold intersection summand has twist −(q−1); the last displayed sum’s lone(−1) is inconsistent when q>2. H_Y^q=0 for q<2.',
 'proof sketched','A','This is the normal-crossing Kummer/purity calculation used in SGA7. The exterior-power ranks and twists are independently checked. The formula is for Rj_* of the divisor complement, whose local degree-one rank is the number of components, not the nearby-cycle rank one less.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S12','kummer')
C(659,[203,205],'poids p+2q−2; d_r=0 si r≥3; dualité.',
 'The local-to-global spectral sequence for H_Y^*(Xbar) has E2^{p,q} pure of weight p+2q−2 under the smooth-projective intersection hypotheses, so only d2 can survive. It degenerates at E3, not generally E2. Duality relates its filtration to the Čech description with the appropriate shifts and total-degree reversal.',
 'proof sketched','B','Weil purity and the algebraic spectral-sequence degree calculation prove the asserted bound. The first possible differential is the Gysin differential; the draft’s duality diagram indices remain scan alarms where the transcription is uncertain.', ['RC_WeilII1980','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S12','weights')
C(660,[205],'R^qj_*Qell=∧^q(⊕Qell_Yα(−1)); poids p+2q.',
 'For the open complement j:U→Xbar of a strict normal-crossing divisor, R^qj_*Q_ell is the q-th exterior power of the Kummer sheaf, with q-fold intersections carrying twist−q. In the Leray spectral sequence E2^{p,q} is pure of weight p+2q, so d_r vanishes for r≥3 and the abutment is filtered by E3 terms.',
 'proof sketched','B','The normal-crossing calculation and weight argument are established with the specified proper smooth strata; the shift and d2 exception are recomputed here. Finite-dimensional numerical checks certify the index formula only.', ['RC_SGA7I','RC_WeilII1980','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S12','weights')
C(661,[207],'X régulier sur trait; X0 NC; mêmes suites; poids et filtration.',
 'For a regular proper scheme over a henselian trait with finite residue field and reduced strict normal-crossing special fibre, the Leray spectral sequence for the arithmetic generic-fibre inclusion has q=0 row H^p(X_s), of weights≤p, and q≥1 rows from smooth proper intersections with twists−q. The weight argument again forces d_r=0 for r≥3; surviving graded pieces are E3, not the displayed E2 terms before taking d2.',
 'proof sketched','B','The distinction between Rj_* for the arithmetic generic fibre and geometric nearby cycles is essential. The former has one local Kummer generator per divisor component; RΨ has the diagonal relation. The rederived weight comparison includes the nonpure q=0 row.', ['RC_WeilII1980','RC_SGA7I','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S12','weights',confidence='medium')
C(662,[209],'d2 motivique; compatibilités à vérifier; polynômes entiers indépendants de ell.',
 'The page expects the d2 maps in the preceding complexes to be induced by algebraic correspondences with the stated compatibilities, and then expects ranks and Frobenius characteristic polynomials of the resulting pieces to be independent of ell, with integral coefficients. The compatibility and the rank/standard-conjecture input are explicitly not checked there.',
 'open on the pages','C','The normal-crossing restriction/Gysin complexes and their weight degeneration are established. The exact unrestricted integrality and ell-independence package asserted here is not a consequence of degeneration alone and was not matched to a theorem proving every one of its quantifiers. The scan index retains the d2 and Tate-twist ambiguities.', ['RC_WeilII1980','RC_Illusie2021'],'RC-S13',confidence='medium')
C(663,[209],'H^i(X_barη)^I et coinvariants purs de poids i.',
 'A literal assertion that both inertia invariants and coinvariants of H^i of every smooth proper generic fibre are pure of weight i is false. For a split Tate elliptic curve over a local field with finite residue field and ell≠p, H¹ has inertia invariants Q_ell of weight0 and coinvariants Q_ell(−1) of weight2, although i=1. The correct monodromy-weight conjecture concerns graded pieces of the monodromy filtration.',
 'false as stated','C','The Tate-curve two-dimensional representation gives a complete counterexample. Weight–monodromy is known in many cases and remains a separate, stronger program; the October2026 Ito–Zou preprint establishes the hyper-Kähler case, not a theorem making this literal purity claim true.', ['RC_Illusie2021','RC_ItoZou2026'],'RC-S13','tate-weights')
C(664,[202],'Prop.2.4: action propre ssi toutes actions de 1-PS non triviales propres.',
 'For a reductive group acting on a separated algebraic scheme over an algebraically closed field under the standing GIT hypotheses, properness of the action can be tested on all nontrivial one-parameter subgroups. The proof uses the valuative criterion and an Iwahori decomposition after the needed extension of the valuation ring.',
 'proof sketched','E','This is reused printed GIT material, not a conjecture attributed to Grothendieck. Mumford’s GIT §2 is the source family; the exact theorem-number/edition collation is recorded in the reference audit. Separatedness and the source’s reductivity convention must be retained.', ['RC_MumfordGIT'],'RC-S14',confidence='medium',authorship='Reused English printed GIT source with handwritten markings; Grothendieck authorship not asserted.')
C(665,[204],'μ additive dans Pic^G; compatible à f*; constant sous limite.',
 'The numerical weight μ^L(x,λ) is additive in the linearized line bundle L, compatible with equivariant pullback, and unchanged upon replacing x by its λ-limit when that limit exists. These statements fix the sign convention used by the Hilbert–Mumford criterion.',
 'stated without proof','E','The fibre-character calculation proves these elementary identities, and the printed source labels their proofs immediate. Their source is GIT §2, not a new manuscript construction.', ['RC_MumfordGIT'],'RC-S14','git-weights',authorship='Reused English printed GIT source; not assigned to Grothendieck.')
C(666,[204],'Th.2.1: semistable μ≥0; stable μ>0, all1-PS.',
 'For an ample G-linearized line bundle on a proper scheme over an algebraically closed field with G reductive in the source’s convention, semistability is equivalent to μ(x,λ)≥0 for every one-parameter subgroup; proper stability is equivalent to μ(x,λ)>0 for every nontrivial one-parameter subgroup. If the trivial subgroup is allowed in the strict inequality, μ=0 makes the literal statement false.',
 'stated without proof','E','This is the Hilbert–Mumford criterion in the reused GIT source. The missing word nontrivial in the stable line is an ink/standing-definition alarm, not evidence of an open stability conjecture. Later reductive-group generalizations must not be retroactively substituted for the edition’s hypotheses.', ['RC_MumfordGIT'],'RC-S14','git-weights',authorship='Reused English printed GIT source; not assigned to Grothendieck.')
C(667,[206],'homogeneous invariant separates0 from closed orbit; finite Z_α cover.',
 'For a linear action in the standing GIT setting, if the closure of the orbit of a nonzero lift avoids0, an invariant homogeneous polynomial of positive degree is nonzero on the lift. Its nonvanishing gives an invariant affine open in projective space. Quasi-compactness supplies a finite family of such opens for the semistable locus in the stated noetherian setting.',
 'proof sketched','E','These are the invariant-separation and affine-chart steps of the printed GIT proof. They rely on the relevant reductivity theorem; this run checks the homogeneous-degree reduction and projective nonvanishing, not a new proof of finite generation of invariants.', ['RC_MumfordGIT'],'RC-S14','git-weights',authorship='Reused English printed GIT source; not assigned to Grothendieck.')
C(668,[206],'properly stable; finite stabilizer and closed orbit.',
 'In the invariant affine open of the source, proper stability is equivalent to a closed orbit with finite stabilizer, or to properness of the orbit morphism. The finite stabilizer is a group-scheme condition; in the source’s characteristic/reductivity convention it must not be replaced by an unsupported claim about smooth stabilizers.',
 'proof sketched','E','This is the geometric orbit condition in GIT §1–2. It is checked as a source identification and hypothesis comparison, not treated as an assertion by the writer of the reverse side.', ['RC_MumfordGIT'],'RC-S14',confidence='medium',authorship='Reused English printed GIT source; not assigned to Grothendieck.')
C(669,[208,210],'Iwahori φ=ψ1 λ ψ2; r_i≥0, puis μ≤0.',
 'After diagonalizing a one-parameter subgroup with coordinate weights r_i and decomposing the valuative witness, regularity forces r_i≥0 on every nonzero surviving coordinate; specializing to0 forces r_i>0. With the source’s μ=−minimum surviving weight convention, this gives μ≤0 or μ<0 respectively.',
 'proof sketched','E','The valuation inequalities and their sign are independently redone. Iwahori decomposition is an external input of the printed argument; it is not proved by the finite weight examples in the certificate.', ['RC_MumfordGIT'],'RC-S14','git-weights',authorship='Reused English printed GIT source; not assigned to Grothendieck.')
C(670,[210,202],'Prop.2.3: nonproper orbit map;0 dans adhérence;1-PS.',
 'For the affine/linear GIT action in the stated setting, failure of properness of the orbit morphism is detected by a nontrivial one-parameter subgroup with a limit, and0 lies in an orbit closure exactly when such a subgroup sends a suitable orbit point to0. The proof begins on210 and ends on202; physical scan order is not argument order.',
 'proof sketched','E','This is the one-parameter-subgroup lemma behind the Hilbert–Mumford criterion. The printed subsection sequence2.10→2.12 and its GIT source were checked separately from the handwritten weights argument on the other faces.', ['RC_MumfordGIT'],'RC-S14','git-weights',authorship='Reused English printed GIT source; not assigned to Grothendieck.')

# Deliberate refinements after the independent Lie and weights audits and the
